    "result_text": "The build is green. `AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class (`AcceptanceBase` is abstract; `AuditLogCapture` is a helper), and it passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers, all within `TelephoneNormalizer` \u2014 the class that already owns the E.164 normalization and country-code logic, so no new catch-all was introduced.\n\n**Change** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneNormalizer.java`):\n\n1. Added a small lookup table `NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS = {\"61\": 9, \"1\": 10}` mapping a country code to its required national-number length.\n2. Added a focused helper `hasValidNationalLength(digits)` that finds the **longest** matching country-code prefix and checks that the remaining national digits are exactly the required length. Unknown country codes are left unconstrained (only the existing generic 8\u201315 digit E.164 bound applies), preserving current behavior.\n3. Wired it into `normalize()` so a wrong-for-country length throws the existing `InvalidTelephoneException`, which `ExceptionControllerAdvice.handleInvalidTelephoneException` already maps to **HTTP 400**.\n\nThis reuses the existing exception \u2192 400 flow rather than duplicating error handling. For example `+61 123` normalizes to `+61123` (national \"123\", 3 digits \u2260 9) and is now rejected specifically for the country-length rule, while valid numbers like `+61` + 9 national digits and `+1` + 10 national digits pass.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
